    without editing code. Costs: more surface than a 30-line webapp needs, and an override reachable by anyone loading
    the page. Genuinely easy to add later if you want it.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m The stated goal is making environment switching easy, and detection is the only
  one of these where switching costs zero steps and can't be gotten wrong by forgetting. The one thing I need from you
  is the real hostnames — without them I'll write [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m/[38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m for dev and leave the staging/prod domains as
  marked placeholders in the spec.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the active environment be selected at runtime? → Hostname detection (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third and last question before I lay out the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the current value is a complete URL with the path baked in — [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m. How you store
  that decides what happens when a second endpoint shows up.

  - [1mBase URL + derived endpoints[0m — each environment stores [38;5;153mapiBaseUrl[39m; the module exports [38;5;153mendpoints.login[39m built from it.
    Buys: adding [38;5;153m/logout[39m or [38;5;153m/profile[39m later means one new line, not one new line per environment. Costs: one extra layer
    of indirection to read through. Changing this later means touching every consumer.
  - [1mFull URL per endpoint[0m — each environment stores the complete login URL. Buys: maximally literal, you see exactly
    what gets called. Costs: the host is repeated in every environment block, so a domain change is an N-place edit and
    the third endpoint makes it a 3×N table.

  [1mMy recommendation: base URL + derived endpoints.[0m It's the same amount of code today and it's the one choice here
  that's annoying to reverse once other code reads from the module — which is exactly the kind of thing worth settling
  before it has consumers.
